module Core

import Native

{-
   The block form of `pushHex` - sixteen bytes read, thirty-two units written, one sixteen-byte table.

   `Format/Hex.yana` beside this states what the operation is and why it is lower case; what is here
   is the half the target decides.

   **The digit is a lookup** - the classic `pshufb` of a sixteen-entry table, which is `lookup` since
   Analysis-Avx512.md §3.3. It used to be arithmetic, `d + 48` with a further `39` above nine, because
   `shuffle` takes a compile-time pattern and there was no operation whose index vector was computed
   at run time; that was a compare, a blend and an add per vector where this is one `pshufb`. The
   table is built once, in front of the loop, and the lookup takes each index modulo sixteen - which
   is what lets the low nibble be the byte itself, with no `and` in front of it.

   **Sixteen lanes, written down, for `interleaveLow`'s reason** - the same one `Core/Read.yana`'s
   digit scan states: the interleave is one instruction at sixteen lanes on every x86 back to SSE2
   and there is none at thirty-two below `avx512`, because every AVX2 shuffle works inside its own
   128-bit half. So a build at `avx2`, where `Vec(U8)` means thirty-two lanes, would not compile this.
   Two `punpck`s turn the high and low nibble vectors into the thirty-two output units in their final
   order. At `avx512` the interleave at thirty-two lanes is `vpermi2b`, and `Hex.native.avx512.yana`
   replaces this body with the same loop at that width - 1.7x when the digit was arithmetic
   (test/bench/avx512/findings.md §5), 1.12x since the lookup made this body 1.4-1.6x faster (§8).

   The write goes into the string's own run rather than through `pushUnit`, which is what the
   reservation is for: the units are provably in range once the capacity has been checked, so the
   two stores are stores. `String.appendUnits` makes the same check for the same reason - a reserve
   the allocator refused is a write past the end of the run and not a lost append.
-}
pub fn pushHex(bytes: [U8], &to: String):
    let n = length(bytes)
    if n <= 0 then return

    String.reserve(to, n + n)

    let start = to.units.length :: Size
    let wanted = start + n + n
    checkCondition((to.units.run.capacity :: Size) < wanted)
    to.units.length = truncate(wanted) :: Count

    let source = bytes.items
    let target = to.units.run.items + start

    let digits = hexDigitVector()

    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i + 16 <= n:
        let block = vectorAt(source + i) :: Vec(U8, 16)
        let highText = lookup(digits, block `shr` (splat(4) :: Vec(U8, 16)))
        let lowText = lookup(digits, block)

        let low16 = bitcast(interleaveLow(highText, lowText)) :: Vec(CodeUnit, 16)
        let high16 = bitcast(interleaveHigh(highText, lowText)) :: Vec(CodeUnit, 16)
        setVectorAt(target + i + i, low16)
        setVectorAt(target + i + i + 16, high16)
        i = i + 16

    -- The ragged end is one more block, read past the end on §5.2's padding, and its units written
    -- up to their count with `storeUpTo` - a write past the run would be the destruction of whatever
    -- follows it. Its `r` bytes are `2r` units: the first sixteen in the low interleave and the rest,
    -- if any, in the high one, whose count is then at or below zero and writes nothing.
    if i < n:
        let block = vectorPast(source + i) :: Vec(U8, 16)
        let highText = lookup(digits, block `shr` (splat(4) :: Vec(U8, 16)))
        let lowText = lookup(digits, block)
        let units = truncate((n - i) + (n - i)) :: Int

        storeUpTo(target + i + i, units `and` 31, bitcast(interleaveLow(highText, lowText)) :: Vec(CodeUnit, 16))
        storeUpTo(target + i + i + 16, units - 16, bitcast(interleaveHigh(highText, lowText)) :: Vec(CodeUnit, 16))

-- "0123456789abcdef" as a vector, for `lookup`: the digits past nine are `a` onwards.
fn hexDigitVector() -> Vec(U8, 16):
    let at = iota() :: Vec(U8, 16)
    let letter = splat(("a" - 10) :: U8) :: Vec(U8, 16)
    let digit = splat("0" :: U8) :: Vec(U8, 16)
    return at + select(at .> (splat(9) :: Vec(U8, 16)), letter, digit)
